From 2f4432f750554997264d4a6d6f7b2ec3b1f91d23 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 04:06:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer, dvbridge: the trim pass of Dolby Vision off by
 default

Where LibreELEC Hades maps Dolby Vision to the display itself (HDR10
adapted to the display, SDR, Low-Latency), the trim pass applied the
level 2 block of the display's peak whenever a stream had one. It is now
drawn only where the new setting "Dolby Vision: apply the trims when
adjusting to your display" (videoscreen.hadesdvtrimpass, expert level)
is on, and that is off by default. Native output does not change: a
Dolby Vision display is still sent the trims unless "Dolby Vision:
ignore the studio's fine-tuning (trims)" (videoscreen.hadesdvnotrims) is
on, and that setting, on, keeps the pass off too.

Why: the normative text does not say on what signal a trim acts (SMPTE
ST 2094-10 6.2.1, equation (1); ETSI TS 103 572 only says how the codes
carry its values). The one place that says it is informative, ST 2094-10
Annex B.3, "Example Transform Using RGB Color Components": linear light,
normalised by the target's peak. The pass follows that example. Measured
against a studio's own grade for a 1000 cd/m2 display (Netflix Open
Content "Sparks", CC BY 4.0: the 4000 cd/m2 master, its colourist's
Dolby Vision metadata of CM v2.9, and the studio's HDR10 version of
1000 cd/m2; six frames of six shots, five with a block for 1000 cd/m2,
all near the identity), the pass took the pictures away from the grade
instead of towards it: mean Delta E ITP (BT.2124) 25.3 without it and
47.2 with it on the BT.2390 curve, 18.1 and 53.0 where the master is
below 100 cd/m2. An offset of -0.0115 is -11.5 cd/m2 in linear light:
every component below it went black. The same equations on PQ values
were not it either (within 1 of no trim on three frames, 2.2 nearer on
one, 8.7 further on another). How Dolby's display management applies
the trims is not published; until it is known, the pass stays available
and off.

- tools/dvbridge: dvbridge_hdr10_mapping::trims_off (why the pass is
  off) and dvbridge_hdr10_mapping_set_trims(), which sets both from the
  two settings. With the pass off, the picture is the same bit for bit,
  and a stream whose block for the display's peak would change it says
  so once (dvbridge_render_notes::trim_off, and why). dvbridge_trims.h
  says what the standards say and what was measured.
- Kodi: the setting, its label and help (en_gb; es_es, es_ar and es_mx
  in Estuary), the help of videoscreen.hadesdvnotrims rewritten for what
  it does now; both mappings take the pass from the two settings; a line
  of kodi.log once per video where a block for the peak is not applied,
  with why; the player information counts level 2 as used in
  Low-Latency only where its pass is drawn.
