@@ -52,6 +52,31 @@
  * ms_weight is reserved in TS 103 572 (a decoder ignores it; the streams at hand carry 2048 there,
  * not the -1 that its text says).
  *
+ * Off by default (dvbridge_hdr10_mapping_set_trims(): drawn only where the user asks for it,
+ * videoscreen.hadesdvtrimpass). The normative text does not say on what signal the trim acts:
+ * ST 2094-10 6.2.1 gives equation (1), y = (min(max(0, (x * S) + O), 1))^P, with x "input value",
+ * and TS 103 572 only how the codes carry S, O and P. The one place that says it is informative,
+ * ST 2094-10 Annex B.3 "Example Transform Using RGB Color Components": "The input pixels are
+ * expected to be the RGB linear light output in the mastering display color volume", E_i the
+ * component mapped by the curve of its equation (7) "in cd/m2", and "The normalization of the input
+ * color components by the TargetedSystemDisplayMaximumLuminance, is necessary for the offset, gain
+ * and gamma tone mapping adjustments. The output adjusted color components are then scaled by the
+ * same factor, producing linear light output in units of cd/m2". That is what this pass does
+ * (dvbridge_trim_apply(), DVBRIDGE_TRIM_GLSL), on the output of the bridge's own curve in place of
+ * that of (7). Measured against a studio's own grade for that target (Netflix Open Content
+ * "Sparks", CC BY 4.0: the 4000 cd/m2 master, the Dolby Vision metadata of its colourist, CM
+ * version 2.9, and the studio's HDR10 version of 1000 cd/m2; six frames of six shots, five with a
+ * block for 1000 cd/m2, all near the identity: S 1.002 to 1.038, O -0.0115 to -0.0005, P 0.990 to
+ * 1.007), the pass took the pictures away from the grade in place of towards it: Delta E ITP
+ * (BT.2124) from 25.3 to 47.2 on average with the BT.2390 curve, from 18.1 to 53.0 where the
+ * master is below 100 cd/m2, from 8.5 to 64.2 there with the spline; an offset of -0.0115 is -11.5
+ * cd/m2 in linear light, and every component below it is black. The same equations on PQ values
+ * normalised to the target's PQ stay within 1 of no trim on three of the five frames, 2.2 nearer on
+ * one and 8.7 further on another: not that either. How Dolby's display management applies them is
+ * not published; until it is known, the pass is drawn only where the user asks for it, and a
+ * stream with a block for the display's peak says once that it is not applied (trim_off of
+ * dvbridge_render_notes).
+ *
  * Header-only, no libplacebo or FFmpeg, so that the host tests include it; the metadata are read in
  * dvbridge_placebo.c (dvbridge_trim_select()), the pass is drawn in dvbridge_render.c.
  */
